{
  "abstract": "A three-cut in a vertex-minimum non-nine-colorable biplanar graph must carry complementary Latin root relations: one side allows exactly the all-equal and all-distinct patterns, and the other allows exactly the three two-equal patterns. We prove that, in the required planar-layer shape, the first side has at least 15 private vertices and the second at least 16. Thus every such Latin pair has at least 34 vertices, improving the earlier 11-per-side bound. The key observation is that every single-root extension of the first private graph, and the private graph of the second side, is nine-chromatic. Gallai's small-order critical-graph bound reduces the first side's 14-vertex equality case to three common-deletion neighborhood families. A degree-table argument excludes them all. We also derive a replacement constraint at every private vertex missed by all roots. Applying positive deletion-coloring transversals to the known 38-vertex,193-edge clique-six source shows that any three-root extension with at most 35 contacts admitting an all-equal coloring also admits a two-equal coloring. This excludes that exact private source from the shaped Latin class. The evidence consists of standard-library checkers for 34 extremal deletions,31,416 neighborhoods,477 literal positive colorings and 1,310,720 transversal subsets. No negative SAT verdict is a theorem premise. These results constrain the Latin-interface construction route; they do not give a general 34-vertex lower bound or solve the Earth–Moon problem.",
